WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.019
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:05.940
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.230
I am Priyanka, and
in this episode,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.010
we will share the
recipe to plan a cold DR

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.650
pattern for applications
that are primarily

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.616
deployed on Google Cloud.

00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:18.096
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.210
Main Street Art has
migrated to Google Cloud,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.760
but they still need a
disaster recovery plan.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.730
They would like to set up
cold DR pattern with one

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.220
recoverable application server.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.610
Based on your architecture,
just one recoverable server

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.870
may or may not work.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.520
But consider this
just as an example.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.710
Here's how this would work.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.810
Create a VPC network.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.880
Then, create a
custom image that's

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.670
configured with the
application service.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.280
As part of the image, make
sure a persistent disk

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.220
is attached for the data
that's being processed.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.790
Then, create a snapshot
from the attached disk,

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:04.540
and configure a startup script
to create a persistent disk

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.140
from the latest snapshot
and mount that disk.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.490
Then create an instance template
from the image we just created.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.680
Using this instance
template, configure

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.090
a regional managed instance
group with a target size of 1.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.510
Make sure the health checks
are configured at the managed

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:24.190
instance group level.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:27.850
Configure internal load
balancing using the regional

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:32.020
managed instance group, and
then configure a scheduled task

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.510
to create a regular
snapshot of persistent disk.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.710
Now, what happens in
case of a disaster?

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.410
Well, Main Street
Art does not need

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.440
to initiate any failover
steps, because they

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.390
occur automatically.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.360
That is the best part of
the default HA features

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.220
available in Google Cloud.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.770
Let's look at some of these
components to learn more.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.320
The load balancer
ensures that even when

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.750
a replacement
instance is needed,

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.540
the same IP address is used to
front that application server.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.490
The instance template
and the custom image

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.890
ensure that the
replacement instance

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:13.250
is configured identically to
the instance it is replacing.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.510
Main Street Art's RPO
will be determined

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.880
by the last snapshot taken.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.370
The more often the
snapshots are taken,

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:23.590
the smaller that RPO value.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.100
The regional managed instance
groups provide HA in depth.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.620
It provides mechanisms to react
to failure at the application,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.150
instance, or zone level.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.850
You don't have to
manually intervene

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.460
if any of these scenarios occur.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.000
Setting a target size of
1 ensures you only ever

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.040
have one instance running.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.010
Persistent disks are
zonal, so snapshots

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.800
are required in order
to recreate disks

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.830
in case of zonal failures.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.380
Although snapshots are also
available across regions,

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:00.430
which permits you to restore
a disk to a different region

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.820
as easily as restoring
it to the same region.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.540
In the event of a zonal failure,
the regional instance group

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.290
launches a replacement
instance in a different zone

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.820
in the same region.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:17.020
A new persistent disk is
created from the latest snapshot

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.750
and attached to
that new instance.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.320
Now, you could use a regional
persistent disk instead

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.050
of zonal, which will be
great, because you won't

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:29.020
have to use snapshots to
restore the persistent disk

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.250
for recovery.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:33.550
But be mindful that
this consumes twice

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:37.020
as much storage, which
needs to be budgeted.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.460
Note that these are
just some scenarios.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.150
The actual decision that
Main Street Art takes

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:48.580
will be dictated by their
budget, RTO, and RPO values.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.180
Well, there you have it.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:52.780
If your application is
deployed on Google Cloud,

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.680
and you are on a specific
budget to meet those RPO and RTO

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.340
values, then use
the cold DR pattern.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.650
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.820
Here's hoping you are whipping
up your own DR strategy.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.490
Join us next time, where
we will share the recipe

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:12.970
to implement warm DR
and HA for applications

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:15.430
that are built on Google Cloud.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.440
If you would like to
see more such content,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.169
don't forget to like and
